WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.380 --> 00:00:09.980
So I said we'd come back to that SE thing with the keyboard screen and the servers the computer within a computer

2
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:17.620
What's that there for that's the support element for the SE in order to get the mainframe all configured and ready to boot up

3
00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:23.780
The way we want it to and connected everything we need to talk to that takes a little bit of a setup and you do that set up

4
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.700
Through the support element

5
00:00:25.700 --> 00:00:29.380
There's two SEs in there and that's just in case one of them fails

6
00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.060
No one wants to be locked out of their system now

7
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:37.540
That's all cool, but imagine if every time you needed to do something you had to walk over to the data center

8
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:46.220
Baj in hang out on the raised floor where it's all loud and cold and dark and stuff open the door slide out the SE and all that

9
00:00:46.220 --> 00:00:48.220
Just to type out a single command

10
00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:57.340
Luckily in addition to the SEs mainframes have this concept of the HMC or the hardware management console and these do four things

11
00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:01.100
One they let you access the SEs functions remotely

12
00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:03.180
So you don't have to walk out there anymore

13
00:01:03.500 --> 00:01:08.380
Two you can add multiple mainframes to a single HMC a hundred of them in fact

14
00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.660
So you can manage a whole bunch of mainframes from one HMC and that is pretty handy

15
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.580
You can also add a single mainframe to multiple HMCs

16
00:01:18.140 --> 00:01:19.420
32 of them in fact

17
00:01:19.660 --> 00:01:25.260
So if you have one system that's shared by multiple departments they can all get HMC access

18
00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:28.780
For you can connect to the HMC remotely

19
00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:33.660
So while the SE is attached to the system and the HMC is running somewhere else

20
00:01:33.660 --> 00:01:37.580
You can also connect remotely as long as you have the correct network access

21
00:01:37.900 --> 00:01:39.580
Now for the remainder of this session

22
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:45.020
I'm just going to be seeing HMC because that's typically how someone accesses those functions

23
00:01:45.020 --> 00:01:47.900
But know that pretty much anything I can do from the HMC

24
00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:52.220
I can do from the SE as well. It's just a whole lot easier just to say HMC

25
00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:54.940
So why would we need that kind of access?

26
00:01:55.020 --> 00:02:01.260
Well, the most common thing people use in HMC for is to stop, start, create and modify L-Pars

27
00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:04.060
You need to add another couple processors to an L-Pars

28
00:02:04.060 --> 00:02:05.900
You do that from the HMC

29
00:02:05.900 --> 00:02:08.460
When it changed the way a network card is configured

30
00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:10.460
You can do that for the HMC

31
00:02:10.460 --> 00:02:14.780
Let's say I'm having problems with a system and I want to get some data that might help me with troubleshooting

32
00:02:15.580 --> 00:02:17.180
Guess what? HMC

33
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.460
What do I see when I log into an HMC?

34
00:02:20.460 --> 00:02:23.420
Well, I can take a look in a particular physical machine

35
00:02:23.420 --> 00:02:25.340
That's this view right here

36
00:02:25.340 --> 00:02:27.660
See you got the status is operating

37
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.900
It's got a machine type model number

38
00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:31.420
It's also got a serial number

39
00:02:31.420 --> 00:02:33.020
It's also got this name over here

40
00:02:33.020 --> 00:02:36.220
So I can make sure it's a system that I want to be talking to

41
00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:39.020
If I go deeper, I can see all the L-Pars

42
00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.660
Those are the partitions right there for that system

43
00:02:41.660 --> 00:02:43.980
Looks like some of them are up, some of them are down

44
00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:45.020
That's all right

45
00:02:45.020 --> 00:02:47.660
And in this last tab over here, that's the topology

46
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:49.660
That shows me how everything is connected

47
00:02:49.660 --> 00:02:51.740
You can see there's lots of stuff going on over here

48
00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:56.860
Another good use of the HMC is just telling me how my systems are doing

49
00:02:56.860 --> 00:02:59.500
So I can see right here there's a couple of exceptions

50
00:02:59.500 --> 00:03:02.620
And an exception is an unacceptable status

51
00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:05.500
And it could be a problem with an L-Pars or any other object

52
00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:07.740
Like a network or a crypto card

53
00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:09.900
These need to be checked out right away

54
00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.980
There's also a number of hardware messages

55
00:03:11.980 --> 00:03:15.580
Now a hardware message is just when some component saw something

56
00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.260
And it wants you to know about it

57
00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.500
But it's not currently stopping anything from working

58
00:03:19.500 --> 00:03:20.540
It's just letting you know

59
00:03:21.020 --> 00:03:24.700
For example, if a fiber cable got disconnected and then reconnected

60
00:03:24.700 --> 00:03:26.380
That would result in a hardware message

61
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:29.020
Same thing if an L-Pars didn't shut down correctly

62
00:03:29.020 --> 00:03:31.420
Or an HMC somewhere lost connectivity

63
00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:33.180
That would result in a hardware message

64
00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:35.740
So what gets stored on the SC

65
00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:39.180
Well, it's got code that's going to get loaded onto the mainframe

66
00:03:39.180 --> 00:03:42.140
That's called licensed internal code or lick

67
00:03:43.020 --> 00:03:43.820
Yeah, really

68
00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:47.420
We've got logging and problem determination functions

69
00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:48.860
Which help into debugging issues

70
00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.180
Hardware systems definitions

71
00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.100
Which basically tell the mainframe how to get started up when it first boots up

72
00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.540
Then there's the IOCDS

73
00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:59.260
Which will definitely be talking about later on

74
00:03:59.260 --> 00:04:02.780
That tells the mainframe how to configure all of its I-O and its L-Pars

75
00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:07.100
There's also a battery operated clock that it uses to synchronize time

76
00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.860
Across all the components

77
00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:10.220
And ethernet adapters

78
00:04:10.220 --> 00:04:12.940
Because of course everything in the mainframe environment

79
00:04:12.940 --> 00:04:15.180
Generally is connected to other components

80
00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:15.900
So we need that

81
00:04:16.700 --> 00:04:18.380
And lastly you might be thinking

82
00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:20.540
So if anyone could log into the HMC

83
00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:23.340
They could probably mess up a whole bunch of stuff accidentally

84
00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:24.060
Right

85
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:24.940
Well yes

86
00:04:24.940 --> 00:04:29.420
Which is why there are several accounts set up by default on the typical HMC

87
00:04:29.420 --> 00:04:32.220
And most people set up even more to partition access

88
00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:33.820
Off to those who really need it

89
00:04:34.300 --> 00:04:37.020
And just because you have access to some features

90
00:04:37.020 --> 00:04:39.260
Doesn't mean you have access to everything

91
00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:41.580
There are specific roles on the HMC

92
00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:44.620
roles like operator and advanced operator

93
00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:48.460
Systems programmer, access administrator and service representative

94
00:04:48.940 --> 00:04:54.140
Each one of these roles has a specific reason for needing specific access to the system

95
00:04:54.140 --> 00:04:56.060
So it's not an all or nothing kind of thing

96
00:04:56.700 --> 00:04:58.940
And that's the SE and the HMC

97
00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:00.940
The computers within your computer

98
00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.340
And within those computers

99
00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.740
There's actually no there's not that's it

100
00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.740
It's just the SE and the HMC
